summary:
One learning loop serves all four writers: A1/S1 Master rewrite, T2 tenant-voice tour rewrite, and T9 blog/social pieces published by T11. The loop runs **capture → mine → gate → release → inject → measure**, and the model weights never change. The writing memory stores two kinds of item: (a) **rules**, short typed instructions or lexicon entries mined from recurring gate failures and from human edit diffs, and (b) **exemplars**, human-approved passages retrieved per writer, channel and tenant and injected as 3 "style only" few-shot examples. Items are scoped **platform** (no tenant text, ever) or **tenant** (stays inside one tenant, under RLS).

An item never reaches a writer on its own. It ships inside a **memory release**, a versioned bundle per writer. A release becomes active only after:
- R0, a deterministic replay at $0;
- R1, a judge-only re-score of frozen outputs;
- R2, a golden-set regenerate (AA-729 plus an edit-pair subset plus a 30% holdout, trial 3 then at most 50 items, capped at $15);
- a shadow replay on recent real inputs that is never shown to anyone.

Hard stops on fidelity (0 new B-class faults) and isolation (0 cross-tenant hits) apply throughout. Rollback means moving the active pointer back to the previous release.

**Phase 0 is urgent, whatever else is decided.** The existing ungated lessons loop is broken: it writes `stage='audit'` at `flag_fix_node.py` L733 but reads `S1_rewrite/S1_editor` at `s1_batch.py` L108-121. It is also dangerous. T2 runs the same graph (`services/content_generation/stages.py`, AA-763), so if anyone "fixes" the loop, tenant `example_before` text would land in the global, tenant-less `shared.pipeline_lessons` and be injected into every prompt.

The work is entirely CIS-owned: new tables go in `shared` (owner `cis_owner`, plain CIS migration numbers after 250, per `docs/database/schema-ownership.md`). The tenant sees only better content. All rules, gates, releases and metrics live under a new admin page, `/admin/writing-memory`.

Estimated effort: about 6 agent-sessions across 6 phases. Running cost on Dev: about $30–50 per month, with no new AWS service.

adr_needed:
Yes, one ADR in the app repo: `apps/AA-CIS-App/docs/adr/0009-governed-writing-memory.md`. The CIS app ADRs currently run 0001–0008 (0006 is stage routes fallback and shadow, 0007 is Jev verdicts may act). It goes in the app repo because reversing the decision would touch only AA-CIS-App: all writers, tables and UI are CIS-owned.

It should cite:
- ecosystem ADR 0002 (only CIS writes content) and ADR 0008 (data platform rules), from `docs/adr/`;
- app ADR 0006 (shadow) and app ADR 0007 (Jev may act).

It should decide:
- (1) no fine-tuning for now. Evidence: Bedrock Claude fine-tuning is EOL, OpenAI fine-tuning is closing on 06/01/2027, and the data volume is too small;
- (2) memory = rules + exemplars, scoped platform or tenant, with the precedence order tenant-authored brand rules > learned tenant > platform, and fidelity gates above all;
- (3) release-gated activation (R0/R1/R2 plus shadow replay, with platform releases approved by Nghiệp);
- (4) it supersedes the AA-132 lessons loop and the AA-99/N8 output rules;
- (5) the tenant-isolation and no-tenant-text invariants.

No ecosystem-level ADR is needed unless TripPlanner or Booking later write content through this memory. Unknown: whether the data-standards catalog in `docs/architecture/data-standards/` requires a front-matter row for the new tables; check this in P1.

mechanism:
**4. Reflective proposer (GEPA-style).** For each target cluster, a reflection LLM receives:
- the current slot body;
- 3–5 member examples with their textual feedback (gate problem, Jev reason, before/after diff);
- the slot's constraints (max_chars, locked-ness, the no-new-claims rule).

The reflection LLM is Claude on acc3 via `LLMClient.generate()`, `stage='opt_reflect'`, role writer. It proposes ≤3 minimal edits as add, replace or retire. Platform-scope proposals see only abstracted, tenant-free examples (entities masked). Tenant-scope proposals see only that tenant's examples. This follows the PREDICT idea: a proposal must explain at least 2 independent member examples.

**6. Holdout confirmation.** The frontier winner runs once on the never-tuned holdout split (~30% of items) of the same golden-set version. A `shared.prompt_eval_runs` row gets `verdict=pass` only if:
- the primary metric improves, or holds while cost falls;
- every hard stop is clear (0 new B-class/C1, 0 tenant-text leak, F10 cross-tenant hits still 0);
- no soft stop fires: re-flag above 8% pooled or up ≥3 points; light-edit-or-better down ≥10 points or under 60%; minutes_to_fix up ≥20%; cost up ≥20%.
